---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import rssTitle from '../assets/images/rss-2025-stage-presentation-title-slide.jpeg';
import rssWide from '../assets/images/rss-2025-presentation-wide-stage-view.jpeg';
import rssAudience from '../assets/images/rss-2025-welcome-screen-audience.jpeg';
import machinePortrait from '../assets/images/mobius-industries-machine-portrait.jpeg';
import workshopDemo from '../assets/images/mobius-industries-workshop-machine-demo.jpeg';

const timeline = [
  {
    role: 'Head of AI, Sensing & Software',
    org: 'Möbius Industries',
    dates: 'June 2025 – Present · New York, NY',
    body: 'Leading the AI, sensing, and software stack behind an automated "de-manufacturing" platform that disassembles and sorts end-of-life electronics and appliances to recover high-purity raw materials, including steel, aluminum, copper, cobalt, and lithium, as an alternative to overseas mining.',
    featured: true,
  },
  {
    role: 'Robotics Researcher',
    org: "MIT d'Arbeloff Laboratory",
    dates: 'September 2024 – June 2025',
    body: 'Built a PyTorch framework translating human force profiles to language and back, ran 10-volunteer human trials, controlled UR 6-axis arms with admittance/virtual-dynamics control, and ran an observational study with a Spaulding Rehabilitation physical therapist. This work became the RSS 2025 and Nature papers below.',
  },
  {
    role: 'Software Engineer Intern',
    org: 'Capital One',
    dates: 'June – August 2024 · McLean, VA',
    body: 'Added Zelle event-data publishing into Capital One’s internal data lakes using Java and Spring Boot, with Snowflake and Splunk for downstream querying and monitoring.',
  },
  {
    role: 'Software Engineer Intern',
    org: 'Apple',
    dates: 'May – August 2023 · Cupertino, CA',
    body: 'Worked on OS-level daemon services in Swift for HomePod and iPhone, plus SwiftUI updates to the Home app. NDA’d, so details beyond this are confidential.',
  },
  {
    role: 'DeFi Research & Backend Dev Extern',
    org: 'Opdefi',
    dates: 'January 2023 · Remote',
    body: 'Researched DeFi protocols (Uniswap, Curve, Convex) and wired up smart-contract interactions with ABIs, typechain, and ethers.js, tested against Foundry mainnet forks.',
  },
  {
    role: 'Backend Engineer (Contract)',
    org: 'Oculo Health',
    dates: 'May – September 2022 · Remote',
    body: 'Built the backend for an AI-powered mental fitness tracker from scratch: an Express.js/Node.js API and a hand-rolled auth system (Passport.js, bcrypt, sessions), deployed on EC2 behind nginx.',
  },
  {
    role: 'Undergraduate Researcher',
    org: 'MIT Mobile Technology Lab',
    dates: 'June – August 2022 · Remote',
    body: 'Backend work on a health diagnostics platform, and began overhauling a legacy audio-sharing site used in mental-health treatment.',
  },
  {
    role: 'Undergraduate Researcher',
    org: 'MIT Signal Kinetics',
    dates: 'June – August 2022 · Remote',
    body: "Serverless backend (AWS AppSync GraphQL, DynamoDB, Lambda) for an IoT indoor-mapping system, within Fadel Adib's wireless-sensing research group.",
  },
  {
    role: 'Undergraduate Researcher',
    org: 'MIT App Inventor',
    dates: 'January 2022 · Cambridge, MA / Remote',
    body: 'Built a new serverless backend, reviewed IoT curriculum submissions from international partner institutions, and built micro:bit-based demos and curriculum projects.',
  },
];
---

<section id="career">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="section-label">Career & Publications</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">Ten roles, two papers, and one origin story worth telling</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      A string of backend, robotics, and research roles over the course of a decade, leading,
      more or less by accident, to a friend's robotics startup.
    </p>

    <div class="timeline">
      {timeline.map((item) => (
        <div class:list={['timeline-item', { featured: item.featured }]}>
          <div class="timeline-role">{item.role} · {item.org}</div>
          <div class="timeline-meta">{item.dates}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          {item.featured && (
            <div class="origin-story">
              <p>
                <strong>How this happened:</strong> Möbius's founder and CEO, Matthew Pierre
                Louis, is a close friend and a fraternity brother from Theta Tau at MIT, a year
                below Karl. Karl had planned to stay at MIT for a Master of Engineering, but
                lost his funding path when federal cuts hit academic research funding. Around
                the same time, he and Matthew were already planning to split an apartment in
                Cambridge. When Matthew asked what Karl's rent budget was, Karl had to admit he
                had no funding lined up and could not actually cover it.
              </p>
              <p>
                Matthew, who was building Möbius and needed a software engineer, offered Karl a
                three-month internship so he could get his financial footing back. Karl ended up
                enjoying the work enough that the three-month internship never really ended. He
                joined full-time the Monday after his Friday MIT graduation and is now Head of
                AI, Sensing, and Software there. As Karl puts it, "the rest is history."
              </p>
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="feature-row" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h3>Life inside the workshop</h3>
        <p>
          Möbius is building toward a planned New York relocation, but the early machines, like
          the "Multi-Line Juliet V2," one of the de-manufacturing line's core sorting units, were
          built and tested out of the Boston/Cambridge workshop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-photos">
        <figure>
          <Image src={machinePortrait} alt="Karl standing arms-crossed in front of the Multi-Line Juliet V2 machine at the Mobius Industries workshop" width={700} height={900} />
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={workshopDemo} alt="Karl gesturing toward a control panel while demoing hardware to a colleague in the workshop" width={700} height={500} />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr class="divider" style="margin: 4rem 0;" />

    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem;">Publications</h3>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Two papers, both on the same throughline: teaching robots to combine physical force and
      spoken language the way a good physical therapist does.
    </p>

    <div class="pub-grid">
      <article class="pub-card">
        <figure>
          <Image src={rssTitle} alt="Karl presenting on stage at RSS 2025, title slide reading Cross-modality Force and Language Embeddings for Natural Human-Robot Communication" width={800} height={500} />
        </figure>
        <div class="pub-body">
          <span class="pub-venue">RSS 2025 · Robotics: Science and Systems</span>
          <h3>Cross-Modality Embedding of Force and Language for Natural Human-Robot Communication</h3>
          <p>
            When two people carry something heavy together, they coordinate with both touch and
            talk: "lift on three," "a little more your side." This project teaches a robot to do
            the same. It learns a shared mathematical space where a physical push and a sentence
            describing that push land in roughly the same place, so the robot can translate
            between "push harder" and an actual force, and back again. Built on a UR robot arm,
            trained and validated with 10 human volunteers, and informed by watching real
            physical therapy sessions at Spaulding Rehabilitation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Presented in person at USC, June 2025. RSS is one of robotics' most selective
            venues, with roughly a quarter of submissions accepted. Karl was a main contributor,
            alongside Ravi Tejwani, John Payne, Paolo Bonato, and Harry Asada, spanning MIT,
            Harvard Medical School, and the Spaulding Rehabilitation Network.
          </p>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02772" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv preprint</a>
            <a href="https://shared-language-force-embedding.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Project site</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/Shared-Language-Force-Embedding/shared-language-force-embedding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code</a>
            <a href="https://roboticsconference.org/2025/program/papers/86/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RSS program</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="pub-card">
        <figure>
          <Image src={rssWide} alt="Wide stage view of the RSS 2025 presentation with two seated panelists" width={800} height={500} />
        </figure>
        <div class="pub-body">
          <span class="pub-venue">Communications Engineering (Nature portfolio) · 2026</span>
          <h3>Adaptive Robot Guidance Through Real-Time Compliance Estimation and Dual-Modal Control</h3>
          <p>
            The direct follow-up to the RSS paper, and the more clinically-minded of the two.
            Picture a good dance instructor: when you're struggling, they physically position
            your arms and give firm verbal correction; once you've got it, they back off to a
            light touch and encouragement. This paper builds a controller that does exactly
            that for a robot arm. It estimates, in real time, how "physically compliant" and
            "verbally compliant" a person is being, and dynamically shifts the mix of force and
            speech accordingly. The approach is proven mathematically safe, meaning it cannot
            inject net energy into the person it is guiding.
          </p>
          <p>
            Validated with a Spaulding Rehabilitation observational study and a 12-person MIT
            user study: up to 50% less tracking error, 39% smoother movement, and 27% faster
            task completion versus fixed-combination guidance. Karl co-implemented the
            controller, designed and ran the experiments, and co-wrote the paper, alongside
            Ravi Tejwani, John Payne, Paolo Bonato, and Harry Asada.
          </p>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s44172-026-00632-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the paper</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/robot-guidance-controller/robot-guidance-controller/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code</a>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.17632/bgybkjtpn9.2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dataset</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="photo-grid" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <figure class="span-12">
        <Image src={rssAudience} alt="Wide auditorium shot of the RSS 2025 opening session at USC, Los Angeles" width={1600} height={700} />
        <figcaption>RSS 2025 opening session, USC, Los Angeles, June 2025</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
